/** @mainpage %Qore krb5 Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section krb5intro krb5 Module Introduction

    The \c krb5 module provides
    <a href="https://web.mit.edu/kerberos/">Kerberos 5</a> and
    <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc2743">GSSAPI</a>
    primitives for %Qore. It is the foundation that lets %Qore code and
    Qorus integration services participate as first-class citizens in
    enterprise Kerberos-authenticated networks — for authentication against
    <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/get-started/virtual-dc/active-directory-domain-services-overview">Active
    Directory</a>,
    <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc4511">LDAP</a>,
    <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc4559">HTTP SPNEGO</a>
    endpoints, databases, message buses, file shares, and any other
    <a href="https://web.mit.edu/kerberos/krb5-latest/doc/basic/index.html">Kerberized</a>
    service.

    @code{.py}
%requires krb5
%requires Krb5Util

Qore::Krb5::Krb5Context ctx();
printf("default realm: %s\n", ctx.getDefaultRealm());
    @endcode

    @section krb5classes Main Classes

    - @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5Context — Kerberos runtime context, cache and
      keytab discovery, initial credential acquisition, credential renewal,
      and TGS service ticket acquisition
    - @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5Principal — Kerberos principal parsing,
      enterprise-principal
      (<a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/ad/naming-properties">UPN</a>)
      parsing, and introspection
    - @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5Credentials — Kerberos credential wrapper and
      metadata
    - @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5CredentialCache — credential cache wrapper
      (file-backed, in-memory, and other backends)
    - @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5Keytab — keytab wrapper for service-key lookup,
      entry management, and entry removal for key rotation
    - @ref Qore::Krb5::GssCredential — explicit GSSAPI credential imported
      from a Kerberos credential cache or client keytab
    - @ref Qore::Krb5::GssClientContext — initiator-side GSSAPI security
      context, target-name type selection, token stepping,
      <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5929">channel
      bindings</a>, and message wrap/unwrap
    - @ref Qore::Krb5::GssAcceptorContext — acceptor-side GSSAPI security
      context for service endpoints, explicit service credentials,
      authenticated initiator-name reporting, channel bindings, message
      wrap/unwrap, and delegated credential extraction

    @section krb5pages Documentation Sections

    - @subpage krb5usecases — how the module fits the %Qore and Qorus ecosystems
      and what integrations it enables
    - @subpage krb5security — the module's security model and guarantees
    - @subpage krb5examples — runnable examples for principal parsing,
      credential acquisition, GSSAPI context stepping, channel bindings,
      message protection, keytab setup, and filesystem-sandboxed usage
    - @subpage krb5releasenotes — release notes and version history
    - the \c Krb5Util %Qore-language helper module
      documentation

    @section krb5util Utility Module

    Use \c "%requires Krb5Util" for %Qore-language helper functions such as
    \c Krb5Util::getHostBasedServicePrincipal(),
    \c Krb5Util::negotiateSpnegoToken() for building
    <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc4178">SPNEGO</a>
    NegTokenInit envelopes, and
    \c Krb5Util::parseSpnegoResponse() for extracting GSSAPI tokens from
    SPNEGO NegTokenResp responses; see the \c Krb5Util module for
    details. Binary classes and constants remain in the \c Qore::Krb5
    namespace.
*/
